      "updated": 1793625749161,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "This diagram presents the multi-protocol ingress architecture of the SmartThings platform. Because IoT devices run on incompatible physical\nlayers and protocols (Zigbee mesh, low-cost Wi-Fi microcontrollers, high-end appliance TCP stacks, proprietary third-party clouds, and mobile BLE\nperipherals), SmartThings does not attempt to force all hardware through a single gateway. Instead, it provides protocol-specialized ingress\ngateways that normalize every device into an identical representation in Device Core.\n\nArchitectural Participants : \n    1. Devices: Zigbee/Z-Wave/Matter hardware, STDK Wi-Fi chips, Samsung OCF Appliances/TVs, Partner Clouds, and Mobile BLE Tags.\n    2. Local Agents: The SmartThings Hub (running Lua Edge drivers) and Galaxy Phones (running the SmartThings App).\n    3.Cloud Ingress Gateways:\n        i. Hub Connection Tier (*.connect.smartthings.com): Handles persistent bi-directional links with physical hubs.\n        ii. Great Gate: High-scale MQTT broker terminating TLS for direct Wi-Fi STDK microcontrollers.\n        iii. OCF Cloud Endpoint: Terminates CoAP-over-TCP/TLS connections for Samsung appliances.\n        iv. Schema Gateway (c2c-{region}.smartthings.com): Communicates with third-party partner clouds.\n        v.REST API (api.smartthings.com/v1): Ingress for mobile devices, webhooks, and third-party apps.\n    4.Device Core & Event Pipeline: The platform center of gravity that stores state and emits state changes.\n\nStep-by-Step Flow & Protocol Mechanics\n    1. Mesh Devices: Communicate over 802.15.4/Z-Wave/Thread radios to a local SmartThings Hub. The Hub's Lua Edge driver parses raw frames,\nvalidates capability schemas, and forwards events over an encrypted persistent TLS link to the Hub Connection Tier.\n    2. STDK Devices: Open an outbound TLS connection directly to Great Gate on port 8883. Authenticate using an ED25519-signed JWT\ncontaining device serial and public key.\n    3. Samsung Appliances: Establish a long-lived CoAP-over-TCP/TLS session to the OCF Cloud Endpoint, publishing their resources under\n/oic/rd.\n    4. Partner Clouds: Exchange state changes and commands with the Schema Gateway using HTTPS JSON REST payloads and OAuth 2.0\nbearer tokens.\n    5. BLE Mobile Devices: Connect via BLE to the user's Galaxy smartphone. The SmartThings app acts as the gateway, calling the REST API\nover HTTPS.\n    6. Convergence: All 5 gateways map their incoming protocol payloads into normalized SmartThings Events ({ deviceId, component, capability,\nattribute, value }) and write them to Device Core, which forwards them onto the Kafka Event Pipeline.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "This diagram presents the multi-protocol ingress architecture of the SmartThings platform. Because IoT devices run on incompatible physical layers and protocols (Zigbee mesh, low-cost Wi-Fi microcontrollers, high-end appliance TCP stacks, proprietary third-party clouds, and mobile BLE peripherals), SmartThings does not attempt to force all hardware through a single gateway. Instead, it provides protocol-specialized ingress gateways that normalize every device into an identical representation in Device Core.\n\nArchitectural Participants : \n    1. Devices: Zigbee/Z-Wave/Matter hardware, STDK Wi-Fi chips, Samsung OCF Appliances/TVs, Partner Clouds, and Mobile BLE Tags.\n    2. Local Agents: The SmartThings Hub (running Lua Edge drivers) and Galaxy Phones (running the SmartThings App).\n    3.Cloud Ingress Gateways:\n        i. Hub Connection Tier (*.connect.smartthings.com): Handles persistent bi-directional links with physical hubs.\n        ii. Great Gate: High-scale MQTT broker terminating TLS for direct Wi-Fi STDK microcontrollers.\n        iii. OCF Cloud Endpoint: Terminates CoAP-over-TCP/TLS connections for Samsung appliances.\n        iv. Schema Gateway (c2c-{region}.smartthings.com): Communicates with third-party partner clouds.\n        v.REST API (api.smartthings.com/v1): Ingress for mobile devices, webhooks, and third-party apps.\n    4.Device Core & Event Pipeline: The platform center of gravity that stores state and emits state changes.\n\nStep-by-Step Flow & Protocol Mechanics\n    1. Mesh Devices: Communicate over 802.15.4/Z-Wave/Thread radios to a local SmartThings Hub. The Hub's Lua Edge driver parses raw frames, validates capability schemas, and forwards events over an encrypted persistent TLS link to the Hub Connection Tier.\n    2. STDK Devices: Open an outbound TLS connection directly to Great Gate on port 8883. Authenticate using an ED25519-signed JWT containing device serial and public key.\n    3. Samsung Appliances: Establish a long-lived CoAP-over-TCP/TLS session to the OCF Cloud Endpoint, publishing their resources under /oic/rd.\n    4. Partner Clouds: Exchange state changes and commands with the Schema Gateway using HTTPS JSON REST payloads and OAuth 2.0 bearer tokens.\n    5. BLE Mobile Devices: Connect via BLE to the user's Galaxy smartphone. The SmartThings app acts as the gateway, calling the REST API over HTTPS.\n    6. Convergence: All 5 gateways map their incoming protocol payloads into normalized SmartThings Events ({ deviceId, component, capability, attribute, value }) and write them to Device Core, which forwards them onto the Kafka Event Pipeline.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
