      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "This diagram depicts Phase 2 of OCF Onboarding: transitioning an unowned device from an insecure factory state into an owned, encrypted, and\noperational state (RFOTM → RFPRO → RFNOP). It establishes cryptographic ownership using the Just Works method.\n\nKey Concepts & Provisioning State Machine\n    1. RFOTM (Ready for Ownership Transfer Method): Device is in factory-default unowned state. Only ownership endpoints are exposed.\n    2. RFPRO (Ready for Provisioning): Ownership established; credentials and access rules are being written.\n    3. RFNOP (Ready for Normal Operation): Device is fully provisioned, locked, and operational (isop = true).\n \nStep-by-Step Walkthrough\n    Phase A: Unsecured Negotiation (RFOTM)\n        Steps 1-2: The Onboarding Tool (OBT) queries /oic/sec/doxm (Device Ownership Transfer Model). The device returns supported methods\n(oxms: [0, 1, 2]) and its permanent UUID (deviceuuid).\n        Step 3: The OBT selects Just Works (oxmsel: 0).\n    Phase B: Anonymous DTLS & Ownership Claim\n        Step 4: OBT and Device execute a DTLS handshake using the anonymous cipher suite TLS_ECDH_ANON_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA256\n(0xFF00). This secures the channel against passive eavesdropping.\n        Steps 5-6: OBT writes to /oic/sec/pstat (Provisioning Status) and writes its own UUID into /oic/sec/doxm as the official owner\n(devowneruuid, rowneruuid).\n        Steps 7-8: OBT provisions security policies: writes initial Access Control Lists to /oic/sec/acl2 and pushes credential descriptors to\n/oic/sec/cred.\n        Step 9: The device derives an Owner Pre-Shared Key (PSK) from the TLS master secret and saves it in privatedata. OBT sets\n/oic/sec/doxm { owned: true }.\n        Step 10: Device updates state in /oic/sec/pstat to dos.s: 2 (RFPRO). The temporary anonymous DTLS session is closed.\n    Phase C: Authenticated DTLS Session & Lock (RFNOP)\n        Step 11: OBT opens a new DTLS session using the authenticated cipher suite TLS_ECDHE_PSK_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA256 powered\nby the newly minted Owner PSK.\n        Step 12: With mutual authentication established, OBT writes final operational ACL rules and sets /oic/sec/pstat { dos.s: 3 } (RFNOP).\nThe device is now securely paired.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "This diagram depicts Phase 2 of OCF Onboarding: transitioning an unowned device from an insecure factory state into an owned, encrypted, and operational state (RFOTM → RFPRO → RFNOP). It establishes cryptographic ownership using the Just Works method.\n\nKey Concepts & Provisioning State Machine\n    1. RFOTM (Ready for Ownership Transfer Method): Device is in factory-default unowned state. Only ownership endpoints are exposed.\n    2. RFPRO (Ready for Provisioning): Ownership established; credentials and access rules are being written.\n    3. RFNOP (Ready for Normal Operation): Device is fully provisioned, locked, and operational (isop = true).\n \nStep-by-Step Walkthrough\n    Phase A: Unsecured Negotiation (RFOTM)\n        Steps 1-2: The Onboarding Tool (OBT) queries /oic/sec/doxm (Device Ownership Transfer Model). The device returns supported methods (oxms: [0, 1, 2]) and its permanent UUID (deviceuuid).\n        Step 3: The OBT selects Just Works (oxmsel: 0).\n    Phase B: Anonymous DTLS & Ownership Claim\n        Step 4: OBT and Device execute a DTLS handshake using the anonymous cipher suite TLS_ECDH_ANON_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA256 (0xFF00). This secures the channel against passive eavesdropping.\n        Steps 5-6: OBT writes to /oic/sec/pstat (Provisioning Status) and writes its own UUID into /oic/sec/doxm as the official owner (devowneruuid, rowneruuid).\n        Steps 7-8: OBT provisions security policies: writes initial Access Control Lists to /oic/sec/acl2 and pushes credential descriptors to /oic/sec/cred.\n        Step 9: The device derives an Owner Pre-Shared Key (PSK) from the TLS master secret and saves it in privatedata. OBT sets /oic/sec/doxm { owned: true }.\n        Step 10: Device updates state in /oic/sec/pstat to dos.s: 2 (RFPRO). The temporary anonymous DTLS session is closed.\n    Phase C: Authenticated DTLS Session & Lock (RFNOP)\n        Step 11: OBT opens a new DTLS session using the authenticated cipher suite TLS_ECDHE_PSK_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA256 powered by the newly minted Owner PSK.\n        Step 12: With mutual authentication established, OBT writes final operational ACL rules and sets /oic/sec/pstat { dos.s: 3 } (RFNOP). The device is now securely paired.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
