      "updated": 1793627337101,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "This diagram illustrates Phase 1 of OCF device onboarding: how the SmartThings app (or Onboarding Tool / OBT) discovers unconfigured or\nconfigured OCF devices residing on the local Wi-Fi/Ethernet network without knowing their IP addresses in advance.\n\nArchitectural Participants\n    1. Client: The SmartThings mobile app acting as the OCF Onboarding Tool (OBT).\n    2. Multicast Group: IPv6 multicast group ff02::158 on UDP port 5683 (OCF All-Nodes Multicast).\n    3.OCF Device: A smart appliance or TV acting as an OCF Server.\n\nStep-by-Step Walkthrough\n    Step 1 (GET /oic/res?rt=...): The Client sends a CoAP Non-Confirmable (NON) multicast GET request to ff02::158 on port 5683. It filters for\nspecific resource types (e.g., ?rt=oic.r.switch.binary) or queries unowned devices via GET /oic/sec/doxm?owned=FALSE.\n    Step 2 (Delivery): The local Wi-Fi router / switch replicates the multicast packet to all network nodes listening on the OCF multicast address.\n    Step 3 (2.05 Content): Matching devices respond via unicast UDP with a CoAP 2.05 Content payload containing a CBOR array of links\n(links[]). Each link exposes an anchor (ocf://<di>), href (resource URI), rt (resource type), if (interfaces), and eps (endpoint IP and port).\n    Step 4 (GET /oic/d and /oic/p): The Client initiates targeted unicast requests to the device's specific IP and port to retrieve the logical\nDevice Identity (/oic/d) and Platform Identity (/oic/p).\n    Step 5 (Identity Metadata Response): The device returns its unique device UUID (di), spec version (icv), manufacturer name (mnmn), model\nnumber (mnmo), and vendor ID (vid). In SmartThings, this vid becomes the presentationId that dictates UI rendering.\n    Step 6 & 7 (GET introspection URL): The Client fetches the OpenAPI 2.0 Swagger specification (encoded in CBOR) directly from the device's\nintrospection endpoint. This tells the Client every custom resource and payload schema the device supports.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "This diagram illustrates Phase 1 of OCF device onboarding: how the SmartThings app (or Onboarding Tool / OBT) discovers unconfigured or configured OCF devices residing on the local Wi-Fi/Ethernet network without knowing their IP addresses in advance.\n\nArchitectural Participants\n    1. Client: The SmartThings mobile app acting as the OCF Onboarding Tool (OBT).\n    2. Multicast Group: IPv6 multicast group ff02::158 on UDP port 5683 (OCF All-Nodes Multicast).\n    3.OCF Device: A smart appliance or TV acting as an OCF Server.\n\nStep-by-Step Walkthrough\n    Step 1 (GET /oic/res?rt=...): The Client sends a CoAP Non-Confirmable (NON) multicast GET request to ff02::158 on port 5683. It filters for specific resource types (e.g., ?rt=oic.r.switch.binary) or queries unowned devices via GET /oic/sec/doxm?owned=FALSE.\n    Step 2 (Delivery): The local Wi-Fi router / switch replicates the multicast packet to all network nodes listening on the OCF multicast address.\n    Step 3 (2.05 Content): Matching devices respond via unicast UDP with a CoAP 2.05 Content payload containing a CBOR array of links (links[]). Each link exposes an anchor (ocf://<di>), href (resource URI), rt (resource type), if (interfaces), and eps (endpoint IP and port).\n    Step 4 (GET /oic/d and /oic/p): The Client initiates targeted unicast requests to the device's specific IP and port to retrieve the logical Device Identity (/oic/d) and Platform Identity (/oic/p).\n    Step 5 (Identity Metadata Response): The device returns its unique device UUID (di), spec version (icv), manufacturer name (mnmn), model number (mnmo), and vendor ID (vid). In SmartThings, this vid becomes the presentationId that dictates UI rendering.\n    Step 6 & 7 (GET introspection URL): The Client fetches the OpenAPI 2.0 Swagger specification (encoded in CBOR) directly from the device's introspection endpoint. This tells the Client every custom resource and payload schema the device supports.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
