      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793628145468,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "This diagram presents the Hub-connected onboarding flow for local mesh radio devices (Zigbee, Z-Wave, Matter). Unlike Wi-Fi and OCF devices,\nconstrained Zigbee devices have no TCP/IP stack. Instead, pairing and protocol translation are delegated to SmartThings Edge drivers written in Lua\nrunning locally on the SmartThings Hub.\n\nArchitectural Participants\n    1. App: SmartThings mobile app UI.\n    2. Cloud: SmartThings Cloud Driver & Device Services.\n    3. Hub (HubCore): Local hub daemon written in Rust and C that controls radio hardware.\n    4. Edge Driver: Sandboxed Lua runtime environment executing device logic on the hub.\n    5. Zigbee Device: Physical 802.15.4 mesh device (e.g., smart plug, door sensor).\n\nStep-by-Step Walkthrough\n    Steps 1-2 (Network Join Window): User selects \"Add device\" in the app. The cloud commands HubCore to open the Zigbee coordinator network for\njoining (permit-join broadcast for 60 seconds).\n    Step 3 (Radio Announcement): The physical Zigbee device powers on, associates with the hub mesh, and sends a Device_annce ZDO message.\n    Step 4 (Descriptor Discovery): HubCore reads the device's Basic cluster, endpoints, and input/output clusters over the air.\n    Step 5 (Fingerprint Upload): HubCore sends the collected hardware identifiers (Manufacturer Code, Model Identifier, Application Version, Cluster IDs)\nto the Cloud Device Service.\n    Note (Matching Engine): The cloud evaluates the fingerprint against available driver packages:\n        1. Exact manufacturer + model match in fingerprints.yaml always wins.\n        2. Generic cluster matches (e.g., standard On/Off Zigbee switch) act as fallbacks.\n    Step 6 (Driver Installation): Cloud instructs the hub to download/assign the selected Edge driver and creates the canonical device record in the\ncloud database.\n    Step 7 (Driver Lifecycle): HubCore instantiates the Lua driver and invokes its lifecycle callbacks in strict sequence: added (first-time creation) → init\n(driver startup) → doConfigure (hardware configuration).\n    Step 8 (ZDO Bind & Reporting Configuration): Inside doConfigure, the Lua driver issues Zigbee Device Object (ZDO) bind requests and ZCL\nConfigureReporting frames to tell the device how frequently to report attribute changes.\n    Steps 9-11 (Initial State & Tile Update): The Zigbee device reports its initial state (e.g., On/Off = 1). The Edge driver converts this into a\ncapability event: device:emit_event(capabilities.switch.switch.on()). HubCore streams this to the cloud, updating the device state cache and reflecting\nimmediately on the mobile app tile.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "This diagram presents the Hub-connected onboarding flow for local mesh radio devices (Zigbee, Z-Wave, Matter). Unlike Wi-Fi and OCF devices, constrained Zigbee devices have no TCP/IP stack. Instead, pairing and protocol translation are delegated to SmartThings Edge drivers written in Lua running locally on the SmartThings Hub.\n\nArchitectural Participants\n    1. App: SmartThings mobile app UI.\n    2. Cloud: SmartThings Cloud Driver & Device Services.\n    3. Hub (HubCore): Local hub daemon written in Rust and C that controls radio hardware.\n    4. Edge Driver: Sandboxed Lua runtime environment executing device logic on the hub.\n    5. Zigbee Device: Physical 802.15.4 mesh device (e.g., smart plug, door sensor).\n\nStep-by-Step Walkthrough\n    Steps 1-2 (Network Join Window): User selects \"Add device\" in the app. The cloud commands HubCore to open the Zigbee coordinator network for joining (permit-join broadcast for 60 seconds).\n    Step 3 (Radio Announcement): The physical Zigbee device powers on, associates with the hub mesh, and sends a Device_annce ZDO message.\n    Step 4 (Descriptor Discovery): HubCore reads the device's Basic cluster, endpoints, and input/output clusters over the air.\n    Step 5 (Fingerprint Upload): HubCore sends the collected hardware identifiers (Manufacturer Code, Model Identifier, Application Version, Cluster IDs) to the Cloud Device Service.\n    Note (Matching Engine): The cloud evaluates the fingerprint against available driver packages:\n        1. Exact manufacturer + model match in fingerprints.yaml always wins.\n        2. Generic cluster matches (e.g., standard On/Off Zigbee switch) act as fallbacks.\n    Step 6 (Driver Installation): Cloud instructs the hub to download/assign the selected Edge driver and creates the canonical device record in the cloud database.\n    Step 7 (Driver Lifecycle): HubCore instantiates the Lua driver and invokes its lifecycle callbacks in strict sequence: added (first-time creation) → init (driver startup) → doConfigure (hardware configuration).\n    Step 8 (ZDO Bind & Reporting Configuration): Inside doConfigure, the Lua driver issues Zigbee Device Object (ZDO) bind requests and ZCL ConfigureReporting frames to tell the device how frequently to report attribute changes.\n    Steps 9-11 (Initial State & Tile Update): The Zigbee device reports its initial state (e.g., On/Off = 1). The Edge driver converts this into a capability event: device:emit_event(capabilities.switch.switch.on()). HubCore streams this to the cloud, updating the device state cache and reflecting immediately on the mobile app tile.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
